19. Retry + Idempotency
Now let's return to the most important problem.
Suppose:
POST /inventory/reserve
Request:
{
  "productId": "IPHONE-17",
  "quantity": 1
}
First attempt:
OrderService
      |
      v
InventoryService
      |
      v
reserve item
      |
      v
database updated
      |
      X
response lost
OrderService sees:
timeout
If it retries:
reserve item again
we have duplicate business effects.
So we introduce a logical operation identity:
reservationId = ORD-1001
Every retry carries:
ORD-1001
The server can then distinguish:
new operation
from:
retry of existing operation
This is the core of idempotency.
________________________________________
20. But idempotency requires durable state
This is where your previous exercise was heading.
Suppose InventoryService does:
check reservationId
reserve item
save reservationId
and crashes:
check
  ↓
reserve
  ↓
CRASH
  ↓
save reservationId never happens
Retry arrives.
The service sees:
reservationId not found
and reserves another item.
Therefore:
The deduplication record and the business state change need appropriate atomicity.
For a relational database, a common approach is to use a transaction plus a uniqueness constraint.
Conceptually:
BEGIN TRANSACTION

INSERT reservation(
    reservation_id = ORD-1001,
    product_id = IPHONE-17
)

UPDATE inventory
SET available = available - 1
WHERE product_id = IPHONE-17
  AND available > 0

COMMIT
with:
UNIQUE(reservation_id)
Now two concurrent requests with:
ORD-1001
can't both create separate reservations.
The database becomes part of your idempotency mechanism.
________________________________________
21. But transactions don't eliminate UNKNOWN
This is crucial.
Suppose:
BEGIN
reserve inventory
COMMIT
The database commits successfully.
Then:
InventoryService
      |
      X
   crashes
OrderService receives:
timeout
What does OrderService know?
Still:
UNKNOWN
The transaction guaranteed:
the database state change was atomic.
It did not guarantee:
the caller received the response.
This distinction is fundamental.
So even with perfect transactions:
operation succeeded
+
response lost
is still possible.
That's why idempotency and retry work together.
________________________________________
22. Retry is not Exactly Once
This is another important concept.
People sometimes say:
"We'll retry until it succeeds, so the operation happens exactly once."
No.
A distributed network doesn't magically give you exactly-once execution.
You can have:
request sent
server executes
response lost
client retries
server receives retry
The operation was executed twice at the transport/request level.
What you can often achieve is something closer to:
effectively-once business semantics
through:
idempotency key
+
durable deduplication
+
atomic state changes
That distinction is important.
________________________________________
23. Java implementation perspective
Now you can understand what a production retry abstraction is really doing.
Conceptually:
Response execute(Request request) {

    Deadline deadline = Deadline.start();

    for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= maxAttempts; attempt++) {

        try {
            return call(request, deadline.remaining());
        }
        catch (Exception e) {

            if (!retryPolicy.isRetryable(e)) {
                throw e;
            }

            if (deadline.expired()) {
                throw new TimeoutException();
            }

            Duration delay =
                retryPolicy.calculateBackoff(attempt);

            sleep(delay);
        }
    }

    throw new RetryExhaustedException();
}
But notice what isn't contained in this code:
Is the operation idempotent?
That's a business/application concern.
A generic HTTP retry library cannot safely know whether:
POST /payment
can be repeated.
That's why production retry design is more than configuring a library.
