11. Connection pool exhaustion
Suppose:
HTTP connection pool = 100
Payment becomes slow.
All 100 connections are occupied:
C1 → Payment
C2 → Payment
...
C100 → Payment
Now request 101 arrives.
There is no connection.
It waits for a connection.
So your request is blocked before it even reaches Payment.
This produces:
Incoming request
       |
       v
connection pool
       |
       X
      wait
Therefore you can have:
application thread available
but:
HTTP connection unavailable
Another form of resource exhaustion.
________________________________________
12. Thread pool vs connection pool
These are separate resources.
Imagine:
200 application threads
100 HTTP connections
Payment is slow.
Potentially:
100 threads
   ↓
waiting on active HTTP calls

100 threads
   ↓
waiting for HTTP connection
Now your service can become unhealthy even though:
CPU = 20%
memory = 30%
This is an important production lesson:
Low CPU does not imply a healthy service.
You can be completely stuck on blocked resources.
