21. Exponential backoff
Suppose you retry immediately:
attempt 1
failure
attempt 2 immediately
failure
attempt 3 immediately
Thousands of clients can synchronize.
Instead:
attempt 1
   |
   | 100ms
   v
attempt 2
   |
   | 200ms
   v
attempt 3
   |
   | 400ms
   v
attempt 4
This is exponential backoff.
A common conceptual formula:
with a maximum cap.
Example:
base = 100ms

retry 1 = 100ms
retry 2 = 200ms
retry 3 = 400ms
retry 4 = 800ms
________________________________________
22. Why exponential backoff alone isn't enough
Suppose 10,000 clients experience the same outage.
They all calculate:
100ms
200ms
400ms
They may still retry at approximately the same time.
This creates synchronized waves:
         retry wave
            ↓
10000 ───────────────>
Then:
another wave
This is the thundering herd problem.
________________________________________
23. Jitter
Add randomness.
Instead of:
delay = 200ms
use something like:
delay = random(0, 200ms)
or another jitter strategy.
Now:
Client 1 → 147ms
Client 2 → 83ms
Client 3 → 192ms
Client 4 → 31ms
...
Requests spread out.
Conceptually:
Without jitter:

|       |       |       |
100ms   200ms   400ms

With jitter:

| |  |    || |   | | | 
This reduces synchronized retry storms.
________________________________________
24. Full jitter vs equal jitter
This is a deeper point.
Suppose exponential backoff gives:
cap = 400ms
Full jitter
random(0, 400ms)
Equal jitter
Something like:
200ms + random(0, 200ms)
These have different distributions.
The correct choice depends on your workload and desired retry behavior.
You don't need to memorize one as universally correct.
The important insight:
Randomness is used to decorrelate independent clients experiencing the same failure.
